  "medium": {
    "heading": "Rodolfos Riddle of the Tens",
    "body": "The medium puzzle today was designed by Rodolfo Kurchan, and you can really feel the step up in logic! The first thing that jumped out at me were the two separate regions that both needed a sum of 10. I immediately set aside my [4,4] and [5,5] dominoes for those spots. I placed the [5,5] at 1,3 and 1,4, which left the [4,4] to help out with the sum of 10 in the bottom right corner.\n\nThe real breakthrough for me was the equals region spanning 2,0, 2,1, and 2,2. Since all three cells had to be the same value, I looked at my remaining high-value pieces. I used the [6,6] domino for the first two spots and then connected the 6-end of the [2,6] piece at 2,2. This worked out beautifully because it also helped satisfy the sum of 6 at the top once I paired the 3-end of the [3,6] piece with the [3,1] domino. It was a bit like a game of musical chairs with numbers, but it all landed in the right place!",
  },
  "hard": {
    "heading": "The Ultimate Grid Marathon",
    "body": "If you thought the medium was tricky, the hard puzzle today really makes you work for it! With 12 dominoes to place, I found myself scratching my head for a few minutes. I decided to focus on the greater than 5 constraints at 2,0 and 4,4. I knew those had to be 6s, so I placed the [1,6] and [3,6] dominoes in those areas first. That move helped me realize that the sum of 5 at the top left had to be a combination of 4 and 1, which led me to place the [0,4] and [4,1] pieces.\n\nThere was a moment in the middle where I almost got stuck on the equals region at 3,0 and 4,0, but once I realized they had to be 2s to match the piece at 4,1, everything else started flowing. The bottom section with the less than 5 regions required some careful counting to make sure I did not use up my smaller numbers too early. Finishing with the [0,5] piece in the bottom corner felt so satisfying. If you are stuck, just remember to breathe and double check those target sums!",
  },
  "tips": "Always start by looking for empty cells and the smallest sum regions first because they act like anchors for the rest of the board. I also suggest keeping track of which dominoes you have already used so you do not accidentally try to place the same piece twice. If you hit a wall, look for the greater than or less than constraints since they usually limit your options to just one or two specific numbers.",
  "learned": "Today I learned how powerful equals regions can be when they are placed near large sum targets. It really narrows down the possibilities quickly once you realize three cells in a row have to share the exact same value. I also noticed that high target sums like ten almost always require specific pairs like double-fives or four-six combinations, which helps clear out the big dominoes early in the game.",
